{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"calcite-cursed-lovebird","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context / Problem\nADR 0049 (`docs/adr/0049-opcert-asymmetric.md`) pins trusted op-cert environment keys in the repository file `.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml`, and ADR 0104 (`docs/adr/0104-mcp-on-box.md`, decision 3 and the env_id note near lines 126-135) builds the MCP server's enforcement plan on that pin file. The operator decided on 2026-11-19 (epic nonblack-brigandish-guillemot) that op-cert keys must not be hardcoded in the repository. This task records that decision as a new ADR before any code changes, so the later stories implement a written decision.\n\n## Approach\nWrite `docs/adr/0125-opcert-trust-per-environment.md` (0125 is the next free number: the highest existing ADR is `docs/adr/0124-jira-fix-versions-release-field.md`, and no open Gerrit change adds an ADR). Use the header shape of ADR 0124 (Status, Date, Ticket, Epic) and sections Context, Decision, Consequences, Alternatives rejected. The Decision section records:\n1. Principle: identity, authorization and op-cert certification are separate. Anyone can sign a plan review, code review or completion verification; signing is never gated on an allowed identity or key; identity can be verified separately.\n2. Default verification is validity-only: the SSHSIG signature verifies under the public key embedded in the signature, for the certificate's principal and the `rebar.opcert.v1` namespace, and the signed subject binds the ticket, kind and material. The result reports the signing key (`signing_key`, a `SHA256:` fingerprint) and the environment (the principal). It does not judge whether the key is trusted.\n3. Optional per-environment trust list: config key `verify.trusted_opcert_keys` (env override `REBAR_VERIFY_TRUSTED_OPCERT_KEYS`), a list of entries `<env_id> <key-type> <base64> [comment]`. It belongs in the verifying environment's own configuration (user config, env var, or deployment), not in the repository. When set, a valid certificate whose (principal, key) pair is not listed gets verdict `untrusted`; a listed pair passes with trust basis `trust_list`. Keys have no eras: removing a key from the list untrusts every certificate it signed.\n4. `verify.require_environment` keeps its meaning (the certificate's principal must equal it), and it now requires a trust list, because a principal is a self-claim; with no list it refuses with `foreign_key`.\n5. `.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml`, `rebar trusted-env`, and the pin file's log-position key eras from ADR 0049 are removed. Signing never consults a trust list.\n6. Rebar's own MCP server is configured through its deployment (`infra/compose/docker-compose.yml` and `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh`) with the 3 current keys, and that config change lands before the code stops reading the pin file, so the live server never runs without its list.\nConsequences cover: review-bot and developer clones become validity-only; certificates signed in other environments read as `untrusted` on the MCP server; the merge gate `rebar verify-opcert` verifies against the gating environment's trust list. Rejected alternatives: keeping the repo pin file; per-key eras in the trust list; gating signing on a pinned key.\nMark ADR 0049 `Status: Accepted; repository key pinning superseded by ADR 0125` and add a one-line note to ADR 0104 decision 3 pointing to ADR 0125. Add the ADR to the `docs/adr/README.md` index in number order.\n\n## Scope\n- `docs/adr/0125-opcert-trust-per-environment.md` (new)\n- `docs/adr/README.md` (index entry)\n- `docs/adr/0049-opcert-asymmetric.md` (status line only; body kept as the historical record)\n- `docs/adr/0104-mcp-on-box.md` (one supersession note at decision 3)\nOut of scope: code, config, deployment and other docs (later stories of the epic).\n\n## Testing\nDocumentation-only; no new testable behavior. Existing `tests/unit/test_opcert_adr_6a89.py` asserts ADR 0049 still contains `trusted_environments.yaml`, `rebar.opcert.v1` and `ADR 0046`; the body is kept, so it stays green. Portability: Markdown only.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `docs/adr/0125-opcert-trust-per-environment.md` exists with Context, Decision, Consequences and Alternatives sections; its Decision names `verify.trusted_opcert_keys`, the entry format, the `untrusted` verdict, the `signing_key` report, the validity-only default, the removal of `.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml` and `rebar trusted-env`, and the separation principle (checked by reading the merged file).\n- [ ] ADR 0049's status line says repository key pinning is superseded by ADR 0125, ADR 0104 decision 3 carries a note pointing to ADR 0125, and `docs/adr/README.md` lists 0125 (checked with `git grep -n 0125 -- docs/adr`).\n- [ ] `make verify` is green, and the change lands through Gerrit with LLM-Review +1 and Verified +1.","id":"02a7-a3ed-f72e-42b1","parent_id":"0dd0-cecb-3932-4c90","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"ADR 0125: op-cert trust is validity-only by default; per-environment trust lists replace repo-pinned keys"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1795112489769239285,"uuid":"b95a8f5a-3abf-4bb6-9351-66ec11c096b3"}